@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the Jain transmission, taken off the register
#
# Taken from traditions/registry-indic.ttl on the testability-driven entrance
# of SPEC 14, and on the structural one at the same time: the registration
# called this transmission the closest thing to a control case inside the
# Indian sphere, because it carries a capacity catalogue of its own that was
# formulated in the same region and period as the yogic one.
#
# What the reading found is not the catalogue the registration expected, and
# the difference is the finding of this file. The capacities this transmission
# names at length are named in prohibitions: the divinatory and the healing
# arts are listed item by item so that the monk may be told to abstain from
# them, and a further and much longer list of operative arts is enumerated in
# order to state what befalls the man who lives by them. What the transmission
# states as obtainable is a chain of inner results with a karmic outcome, and
# it states that chain seventy-three times over in one lecture.
#
# Two properties of the material bear on examination and are entered as their
# own nodes rather than left as an impression. The failure of the practice is
# located in the practitioner and carries no outward mark: four passages in
# three works say that the tokens of the order, the nakedness, the shaven
# crown and the month-long fast avail nothing where deceit is present. And
# nothing in what was read names a perceptible sign by which the practitioner
# would know that a practice has worked. That second one is a search and not
# an impression; it stands in examinations/searches-jain.ttl.
#

pmt:Jain a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Jain transmission"@en, "Jainistische Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Jain transmission of restraint, austerity and the shedding of karmic matter, with its own catalogue of powers said to arise along the way."@en ;
    skos:note "A second capacity list formulated in the same region and period as the yogic one and not derived from it. Within the Indian sphere this is the closest thing to a control case."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Ācārāṅga Sūtra; Umāsvāti, Tattvārthasūtra; Hemacandra, Yogaśāstra" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    skos:note "Read in two public-domain English translations from the Prakrit, both read at the wording of the translation and not at the Prakrit. First, Jacobi's Jaina Sūtras Part II (Sacred Books of the East 45, Oxford 1895): the Uttarādhyayana lectures V, XV, XVII, XX, XXIX, XXX and XXXV read in full, XXXVI searched and read at the hits; the Sūtrakṛtāṅga at I.2.1, I.7, I.12 and II.2.25-27. Second, Jacobi's Jaina Sūtras Part I (Sacred Books of the East 22, Oxford 1884): the Ācārāṅga at I.7.6 to I.7.8 and I.8.4, at II.13, and the Kalpa Sūtra at 117 and 120 with its parallel at Ācārāṅga II.15.23-24."@en ;
    skos:note "What was not read, and it is the larger part of both volumes and the whole of the principal corpus as the registration named it. The Tattvārthasūtra and Hemacandra's Yogaśāstra were not opened: the first has two recensions with divergent sūtra numbering and the second exists in English only in a modern edition that is not open access, and a claim citing either would have to say which numbering it follows. Of the Ācārāṅga, the first book outside lectures 7 and 8 and almost the whole of the second were not read. Of the Uttarādhyayana, twenty-eight of the thirty-six lectures were not read in full. The Sūtrakṛtāṅga was read at four places out of two books. The dense capacity catalogues the landscape stage named — the Aupapātika, the Tiloyapaṇṇatti, the Pravacanasāroddhāra — have no public-domain English translation at all, which is a property of those works and is why they are not here."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route stays documented and the reading did not move it. The transmission shares its sphere, its period and much of its vocabulary with the material this holding already carries from India, and it names the same arts the Atharvavedic and yogic files name. What the reading did establish is that the agreement is at the level of the list and not of the doctrine: where the neighbouring transmissions promise these arts, this one forbids them and states a penalty for practising them."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX (Hermann Jacobi, Jaina Sūtras Part II, Sacred Books of the East 45, Oxford: Clarendon 1895; read in the Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

#
# The catalogue of results: the one lecture that states the whole path as a
# chain of question and answer
#

pmc:JainaSammattaparakkama a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The exertion in righteousness, stated as seventy-three questions"@en, "Das Streben in der Rechtschaffenheit, als dreiundsiebzig Fragen gestellt"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "sammattaparakkama"@en, "samyaktvaparākrama"@en ;
    skos:definition "The form the twenty-ninth lecture takes: a numbered list of seventy-three items, each put as a question and answered in the same words. Sir, what does the soul obtain by X? By X the soul obtains Y. The list runs from the longing for liberation and the disregard of worldly objects through confession, study, control and austerity to the renunciation of company, of food and of the body, and ends at the straight upward motion of the freed soul."@en ;
    skos:note "The question form is the point and not the ornament. A transmission that states its path as a list of what each step obtains has committed itself to an answer at every step, and each answer can be read on its own and weighed against the next. The translation drops the question from the third item onward for brevity and says so; the lecture carries it throughout."@en ;
    skos:note "The answers are almost always chained: X obtains Y, and by Y he obtains Z. Eleven of the seventy-three end in the same terminal formula, that he obtains perfection, enlightenment, deliverance and final beatitude and puts an end to all misery. What the chain never does is name a perceptible event, and that is the subject of the search at examinations/searches-jain.ttl."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX, with the list of subjects at its head and the seventy-three answers following (Hermann Jacobi, Jaina Sūtras Part II, Sacred Books of the East 45, Oxford: Clarendon 1895; read in the Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmp:JainaSamvega a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The longing for liberation"@en, "Das Verlangen nach Erlösung"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "saṃvega"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first item of the lecture: the longing for liberation, held as something the soul does rather than something that befalls it, since the lecture asks what the soul obtains by it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.1 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaTribhavaAvadhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Perfection within three births at the most"@en, "Vollendung in höchstens drei Geburten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the first item states, and it states it as a bound: by the purity of faith some will reach perfection after one birth, and nobody who has got this purity will be born more than thrice before he reaches perfection."@en ;
    skos:note "The one claim in the lecture that a count could contradict, and the only bound anywhere in what was read that is stated as a maximum rather than as a promise. It is not open to a count that anybody could carry out, because the unit is a birth; the shape is nevertheless the shape of a falsifiable statement, and it is worth holding as the case where a transmission put an upper limit on its own remedy."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.1 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaSamvegaYieldsBoundedRebirth a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The longing for liberation bounds the number of births"@en, "Das Verlangen nach Erlösung begrenzt die Zahl der Geburten"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaSamvega ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaTribhavaAvadhi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:note "The chain in the text runs through two further terms before it arrives at the bound: the longing obtains an intense desire of the Law, the desire destroys anger, pride, deceit and greed, the soul acquires no bad Karman and becomes possessed of right faith, and the bound is stated of the purity of that faith. The node names the two ends of the chain and the source carries the middle."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.1 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmp:JainaAlocana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Confession of sins before the teacher"@en, "Sündenbekenntnis vor dem Lehrer"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "ālocanā"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fifth item: the confession of one's sins in the presence of the Guru, distinguished in the same list from repenting of them to oneself and from repenting of them before the Guru, which are the sixth and the seventh."@en ;
    skos:note "Three neighbouring items that a summary would collapse into one. The lecture keeps them apart and gives each a different result: confession removes the thorns, self-repentance produces an ascending scale of virtues, repentance before the teacher produces humiliation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.5-7 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaTrisalyaMukti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Freedom from the three thorns"@en, "Freiheit von den drei Dornen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The result the fifth item states: the soul gets rid, as it were of thorns, of deceit, of misapplied austerities and of wrong belief, which obstruct the way to final liberation and cause an endless migration of the soul; and it obtains simplicity, so that the soul free from deceit acquires no Karman resulting in carnal desire and annihilates such as it had acquired before."@en ;
    skos:note "Misapplied austerity stands in the list of what confession removes, and the translator names the term behind it, the austerity undertaken for a reward. A transmission that counts a wrongly motivated austerity among the obstacles alongside deceit and wrong belief has said that its own principal practice can be performed in a way that adds to what it is meant to remove."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.5 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaAlocanaYieldsFreedomFromThorns a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Confession removes the three thorns"@en, "Das Bekenntnis entfernt die drei Dornen"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaAlocana ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaTrisalyaMukti ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.5 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmp:JainaAnupreksha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Pondering on what has been learned"@en, "Das Nachsinnen über das Gelernte"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "anuprekṣā"@en ;
    skos:definition "The twenty-second item, one of the five parts into which the same lecture and the thirtieth divide study: saying or learning the lesson, questioning the teacher, repetition, pondering, and religious discourse."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.22, with the fivefold division at XXX.34 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaKarmasthitiHrasa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The duration of the karman shortened and its power mitigated"@en, "Die Dauer des Karman verkürzt und seine Kraft gemildert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The result the twenty-second item states: the soul loosens the firm hold which seven of the eight kinds of Karman have upon it, all but the one that determines the length of life; it shortens their duration where it was to be a long one and mitigates their power where it was intense; it may either acquire the life-determining Karman or not, but it accumulates no more of the kind that produces unpleasant feelings."@en ;
    skos:note "The exception is what makes the claim worth holding. Seven kinds are said to be shortened and the eighth, the one fixing the span of the present life, is named and excluded. A transmission that exempts exactly the quantity an outsider could measure has drawn the line where a test would have to begin, and it has drawn it explicitly rather than by silence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.22, with the eight kinds of Karman at XXXIII.2-3 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaAnuprekshaYieldsShortening a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Pondering shortens the karman"@en, "Das Nachsinnen verkürzt das Karman"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaAnupreksha ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaKarmasthitiHrasa ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.22 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmp:JainaVaiyavrittya a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Doing service to the ten"@en, "Der Dienst an den zehn"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "vaiyāvṛttya"@en ;
    skos:definition "The forty-third item: giving one's assistance as well as one is able, in ten kinds according to whom it is rendered to, the teacher being the first of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.43, with the ten kinds at XXX.33 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaTirthakaranamagotra a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The karman that brings about the name and family of a Tīrthakara"@en, "Das Karman, das Namen und Geschlecht eines Tīrthakara bewirkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The result the forty-third item states, and the only item of the seventy-three whose result is a particular station rather than a removal: by doing service the soul acquires the Karman which brings about for him the name and the family of a Tīrthakara."@en ;
    skos:note "Worth keeping beside the rest because it runs the other way. The lecture otherwise states results as subtractions — Karman destroyed, obstacles removed, births not taken. Here a Karman is acquired on purpose, and the station it produces is the one the transmission reserves for the founders of its own order."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.43 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaVaiyavrittyaYieldsTirthakaragotra a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Service brings about the station of a Tīrthakara"@en, "Der Dienst bewirkt die Stellung eines Tīrthakara"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaVaiyavrittya ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaTirthakaranamagotra ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.43 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmp:JainaBhaktapratyakhyana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Renouncing all food"@en, "Der Verzicht auf alle Nahrung"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "bhaktapratyākhyāna"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fortieth item of the lecture, and the first and least severe of the three methods of the religious death in the Ācārāṅga: lying down without food, examining the ground and spreading straw on it, bearing the pains that attack, not killing the animals that feed on the body and not rubbing the wound, and not stirring from the position while they destroy it."@en ;
    skos:note "The same act stands in both works and is entered once. The Uttarādhyayana names it in the list of what obtains a result; the Ācārāṅga describes how it is carried out and what distinguishes it from the two harder methods."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.40 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)", "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.7-11 (Hermann Jacobi, Jaina Sūtras Part I, Sacred Books of the East 22, Oxford: Clarendon 1884; read in the Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaJanmashatanivarana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Many hundreds of births prevented"@en, "Viele Hunderte von Geburten verhindert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The result the fortieth item states in one clause and without qualification: by renouncing all food he prevents his being born again many hundreds of times."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.40 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaBhaktapratyakhyanaYieldsFewerBirths a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The renunciation of all food prevents hundreds of births"@en, "Der Verzicht auf alle Nahrung verhindert Hunderte von Geburten"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaBhaktapratyakhyana ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaJanmashatanivarana ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.40 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaYoganirodhakrama a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Mind, then speech, then body, then the breath"@en, "Erst der Geist, dann die Rede, dann der Leib, zuletzt der Atem"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:JainaPanchahrasvaksharakala ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JainaBhaktapratyakhyana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:definition "The order the seventy-second item states for the last stage: he first stops the functions of his mind, then the functions of speech, then those of the body, and at last he ceases to breathe."@en ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, and the reason is the same throughout this file: the lecture names the sequence and never names what happens to the man who takes it in another order. The strength is set at what the wording carries and the search for a stated consequence of omission stands at examinations/searches-jain.ttl."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.72 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaPanchahrasvaksharakala a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The time required for pronouncing five short syllables"@en, "Die Zeit, die das Aussprechen von fünf kurzen Silben braucht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The measure the seventy-second item gives for the final meditation: when his life is spent up to less than half a muhūrta he discontinues to act, and during the time required for pronouncing five short syllables he is engaged in the final pure meditation, in which all functions have ceased and the four remaining kinds of Karman are annihilated simultaneously."@en ;
    skos:note "Two measures in one sentence, one of them stated in a unit of the calendar and one in an act of speech, and both are shorter than anything else the lecture measures. A transmission that fixes the length of its own last operation by how long it takes to say five syllables has made a statement of a kind nothing else in this file makes, and it has made it about the one moment no witness is present for."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXIX.72 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

#
# Austerity as a counted procedure, and the founder's own regimen
#

pmc:JainaDvadashaTapas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The twelve austerities, six outward and six inward"@en, "Die zwölf Askesen, sechs äußere und sechs innere"@de ;
    skos:definition "The division the thirtieth lecture sets out. The six external: fasting, abstinence, collecting alms, abstention from dainty food, mortification of the flesh, and taking care of one's limbs. The six internal: expiation of sins, politeness, serving the teacher, study, meditation, and the abandoning of the body."@en ;
    skos:note "The count is the transmission's own and the translator notes that the general division agrees with the other canonical list he compares it to while the subdivisions differ in many details. The list is entered as this lecture gives it and not harmonised."@en ;
    skos:note "Expiation stands inside austerity here and not beside it. That places the whole apparatus of penances for the ascetic's own failures among the practices that shed Karman, which means that in this transmission the correction of a failed observance is itself an observance."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXX.7-8 and XXX.30-36 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmp:JainaAnasanaItvara a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Temporary fasting in six counted arrangements"@en, "Zeitweiliges Fasten in sechs gezählten Anordnungen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "itvara"@en, "anaśana"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first of the external austerities in its temporary form, divided into six kinds by the figure the successive fasts make: in the form of a line, of a square, of a cube, of a sixth power, of a twelfth power, and of any arrangement. The lecture adds that temporary fasting can be practised for different objects which one has in mind, and distinguishes the form in which a desire for food is present from the form in which none is."@en ;
    skos:note "A counted practice whose count is stated as a figure rather than as a number of days, and the only place in what was read where the transmission builds a schedule out of an arithmetic. The translator supplies the arithmetic from the commentary and remarks that the last two classes would require hundreds of thousands of years; that computation is his and the commentary's, and only the six names and the two distinctions are entered here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXX.9-11 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmp:JainaMahaviraUpadhana a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The founder's own regimen of fasting and exposure"@en, "Das eigene Regime des Stifters aus Fasten und Aussetzung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The regimen the ninth lecture of the Ācārāṅga reports of Mahāvīra in the first person of the tradition: in the cold season he meditated in the shade, in summer he exposed himself to the heat and sat squatting in the sun; he lived on rice, pounded jujube and beans, and sustained himself on these three for eight months; sometimes he did not drink for half a month or a month, sometimes for more than two months or even six months, day and night; sometimes he ate only the sixth meal, or the eighth, the tenth, the twelfth."@en ;
    skos:note "The counted meal is the unit and not the day: the sixth, the eighth, the tenth, the twelfth meal. That is the same kind of count the temporary fast is built from and it is the form in which this transmission states a quantity when it states one at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.8.4.3-7 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaMahaviraCikitsanirodha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The founder desired no medical treatment"@en, "Der Stifter begehrte keine Heilbehandlung"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the same lecture states of him immediately before the regimen: he was able to abstain from indulgence of the flesh though never attacked by diseases, and whether wounded or not wounded he desired no medical treatment; purgatives and emetics, anointing of the body and bathing, shampooing and cleansing of the teeth do not behove him, after he learned that the body is something unclean."@en ;
    skos:note "The list of what does not behove him is the same list, item for item, that the fifteenth lecture of the Uttarādhyayana forbids the monk to practise on others. The refusal to be treated and the prohibition of treating are two sides of one rule in this transmission, and both are entered here because only the second was expected."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.8.4.1-2 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmp:JainaDvadashavarshaNirodha a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The twelve years of neglecting the body"@en, "Die zwölf Jahre der Vernachlässigung des Leibes"@de ;
    skos:definition "The resolution the Kalpa Sūtra and the fifteenth lecture of the second book of the Ācārāṅga put in the founder's mouth on entering the order: I shall for twelve years neglect my body and abandon the care of it; I shall with equanimity bear, undergo and suffer all calamities arising from divine powers, men or animals."@en ;
    skos:note "A term the practitioner sets himself before he begins, stated as a number of years and not as a condition to be reached. It is the only practice in what was read whose duration is fixed in advance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Kalpa Sūtra 117, with the parallel at Ācārāṅga Sūtra II.15.23-24 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaKevalaprapti a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The attainment dated to the muhūrta"@en, "Die auf die Muhūrta datierte Erlangung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The event the Kalpa Sūtra reports with every circumstance it has: during the thirteenth year, in the second month of summer, in the fourth fortnight, the light fortnight of Vaiśākha, on its tenth day called Suvrata, in the muhūrta called Vijaya, outside the town Jṛmbhikagrāma on the bank of the river Ṛjupālikā, not far from an old temple, in the field of the householder Sāmāga, under a Sāl tree, while the moon was in conjunction with the asterism Uttaraphalgunī, when the shadow had turned towards the east and the first wake was over, in a squatting position with joined heels, exposing himself to the heat of the sun, after fasting two and a half days without drinking water, being engaged in deep meditation, he reached the highest knowledge and intuition, called Kevala."@en ;
    skos:note "The most fully specified single attainment in what was read, and the specification is of everything except the man. Year, month, fortnight, day, muhūrta, asterism, direction of the shadow, watch of the day, place, tree, posture, exposure and the length of the immediately preceding fast are all named; nothing is said of any perceptible mark by which the attainment announced itself, and the account gives none afterwards either."@en ;
    skos:note "The two and a half days without water are the one operative condition in the sentence that a later reader could repeat. Everything else in it is a coordinate."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Kalpa Sūtra 120, with the parallel at Ācārāṅga Sūtra II.15.25 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaDvadashavarshaBeforeKevala a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The twelve years stand before the attainment"@en, "Die zwölf Jahre stehen vor der Erlangung"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:JainaKevalaprapti ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JainaDvadashavarshaNirodha ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:note "Presentation order and nothing stronger, deliberately. The work narrates the twelve years and then the thirteenth year in which the attainment fell; it does not say that twelve years are required, and it states no consequence for a shorter term. The introduction to the edition read asserts that twelve years of penance were always held essential and that every ascetic has to undergo a course of that length; that is the editor writing in 1884 and it is recorded here as his and not entered as the transmission's."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kalpa Sūtra 117 and 120 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

#
# The death by fasting: its entry condition, its abort condition, its grades,
# and the two states that spoil it
#

pmp:JainaSamlekhanaItvara a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The straw spread for the religious death"@en, "Das für den religiösen Tod ausgebreitete Stroh"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "itvara"@en ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the sixth lesson prescribes. If the thought occurs to a monk that he is sick and not able at this time to regularly mortify the flesh, that monk should regularly reduce his food, and reducing his food and diminishing his sins should take proper care of his body, being immovable like a beam. He should then beg for straw, retire with it to a secluded spot, repeatedly examine and clean the ground where there are no eggs, living beings, seeds, sprouts, dew, water, ants, mildew, waterdrops, mud or cobwebs, spread the straw on it, and there and then effect the death called itvara."@en ;
    skos:note "The entry condition is an incapacity and not an achievement: the procedure begins where the ordinary austerity can no longer be carried on. The transmission therefore states the end of its own principal practice as the occasion for its last one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.6.3-4 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaGlaneAharaPunah a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "If the mendicant falls sick, let him take food again"@en, "Wird der Mönch krank, so nehme er wieder Nahrung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The clause that stands in the middle of the instruction for the death by fasting: subduing the passions and living on little food he should endure hardships, and if a mendicant falls sick, let him again take food."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:JainaSamlekhanaItvara ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:note "An abort condition written into the prescription itself, and the rarest thing in this holding: an instruction that names the circumstance under which it is to be broken off, inside the same passage that gives it. It is not a warning about a danger but a rule for stopping, and it is addressed to the practitioner rather than to anyone watching him."@en ;
    skos:note "It sits beside a second clause with the same direction, four verses later: whatever means one knows for calming one's own life, that a wise man should learn in order to gain time for continuing penance. The two together make the preservation of the life a duty of the man who is fasting to death, which is the point at which this practice separates from the act it otherwise resembles."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.3, with I.7.8.6 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaJivitamaranaAkanksha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The longing for life and the wish for death"@en, "Das Verlangen nach Leben und der Wunsch nach Tod"@de ;
    skos:definition "The two states the eighth lesson excludes in one line: he should not long for life, nor wish for death; he should yearn after neither, life or death. The next verse says of the man who is indifferent and wishes for the destruction of Karman that he should continue his contemplation."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition on the act is a state of the man and not an act of his, and there is nothing in the passage by which a bystander could tell whether it holds. That is the same shape this holding carries from the Chinese medical canon, where a failure is located in the patient without a mark that was recognisable beforehand, and it is worth noting that here the transmission does it to the practitioner rather than to the person practised on."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.4-5 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstWishing a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Yearn after neither life nor death"@en, "Verlange weder nach Leben noch nach Tod"@de ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaJivitamaranaAkanksha ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:note "Entered beside the further clause at the end of the same lesson, which forbids attachment to the transitory pleasures and to the greater ones and forbids nourishing desire and greed. The translation of that verse is difficult at its last words and the difficulty is left visible rather than smoothed: what stands in the edition read is that he should not nourish desire and greed, looking only for eternal praise, and whether the Prakrit forbids the seeking of praise or enjoins it is a question for the critical edition and not for this node."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.4, with I.7.8.23 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaDevamaya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The delusive power of the gods"@en, "Die trügerische Macht der Götter"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the twenty-fourth verse of the eighth lesson tells the dying man not to trust: he should be enlightened with eternal objects and not trust in the delusive power of the gods."@en ;
    skos:note "A warning about an appearance during the practice, and the only one in what was read. It says that something may be seen or offered in the course of the fast that is not what it presents itself as, and it gives no mark by which the practitioner should tell the one from the other. Held as the counterpart of the search for a sign of success: this transmission names no perceptible sign of the practice working and does name a perceptible appearance that must not be believed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.24 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstDevamaya a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Do not trust the delusive power of the gods"@en, "Traue der trügerischen Macht der Götter nicht"@de ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaDevamaya ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.24 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaTrividhamaranakrama a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Three deaths in an ascending order of difficulty"@en, "Drei Sterbeweisen in aufsteigender Schwierigkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The grading the eighth lesson states in its own words as it passes from one method to the next. The first is the fast without food in which the man may bear the pains where he lies. Of the second the text says that a more exalted method follows for a well-controlled and instructed monk, in which he gives up all motions except his own and may walk, stand or sit as he tires. Of the third it says that this is a still more difficult method, not to stir from one's place while checking all motions of the body, and that this is the highest law, exalted above the preceding method."@en ;
    skos:note "The order is the text's and not a reader's: each step is introduced by a comparative. A transmission that ranks its own terminal procedures by difficulty has stated something that can be held against its own reports of who performed which, which is what makes the grading worth a node of its own."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.7-25, with the comparatives at I.7.8.11, 19 and 20 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

pmc:JainaSamyamaJnanaPurva a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Control and knowledge come before the religious death"@en, "Beherrschung und Wissen gehen dem religiösen Tod voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:JainaTrividhamaranakrama ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:JainaBhaktapratyakhyana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:definition "The condition the lesson opens with: the wise ones who attain in due order to one of the unerring states, those who are rich in control and endowed with knowledge, knowing the incomparable religious death, should continue their contemplation."@en ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity: the verse states who does this and in what condition, and names nothing that befalls the man who does it otherwise. The edition read carries in a footnote the scholiast's rule that the death is normally permitted only after twelve years of preparatory penance and that the ascetic must ask and get the permission of his teacher; both are the commentary and neither stands in the verses, so neither is entered."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra I.7.8.1-2 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .

#
# Where the failure is located, and the mark it does not have
#

pmc:JainaLingamNaTrayate a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The outward tokens will not save a sinful ascetic"@en, "Die äußeren Zeichen retten den sündigen Asketen nicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The statement four passages of three works make in the same terms. Bark and skin of a goat, nakedness, twisted hair, baldness: these outward tokens will not save a sinful ascetic, and a sinner though he be a mendicant will not escape hell. He who walks about naked and lean, he who eats only once after a month, if he is filled with deceit, will be born an endless number of times. One who for a long time wears a shaven crown and mortifies himself, but who is careless with regard to the vows and neglects penance and self-control, will not be a winner in the battle; he is empty like a clenched fist and like an uncoined false coin. In vain he adopts nakedness, who errs about matters of paramount interest."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the finding the file was worth opening for, and it is repeated too often to be an aside. The transmission prescribes nakedness, the plucked or shaven head and the month-long fast, and then states in four places that a man may have all of them and be nowhere. What decides is named as deceit, carelessness about the vows and error about what matters, and none of the three has an outward mark anywhere in what was read."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence for examination is that this transmission cannot be embarrassed by a case. Any practitioner who fails is, on these verses, a practitioner who was filled with deceit, and the verses supply no way of having known that beforehand. The same clause protects the promise and empties it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra V.21-22 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)", "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XX.41-43 and XX.49 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)", "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.2.1.9 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstOutwardTokens a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Against taking the badge for the thing"@en, "Gegen das Verwechseln des Abzeichens mit der Sache"@de ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaLingamNaTrayate ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:note "A self-warning in the strict sense: the text warns against the very marks it prescribes, and it does so without withdrawing the prescription."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra V.21-22 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)", "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.2.1.9 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaSakamamaranaNaSarvesham a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The good death does not fall to every monk"@en, "Der gute Tod fällt nicht jedem Mönch zu"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the fifth lecture says after distinguishing the death against one's will from the death with one's will: such a death does not fall to the lot of every monk, nor of every householder, for the morality of householders is of various character and that of monks is not always good throughout; and some householders are superior to some monks in self-control, though the saints are superior to all householders."@en ;
    skos:note "An explicit refusal of the guarantee, stated by the transmission about its own order. It is also the passage that opens the good death to the layman, which means the outcome is not reserved to those who carry the marks and cannot be read off them in either direction."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra V.19-20 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaPapashramana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The bad ascetic, drawn in twenty marks"@en, "Der schlechte Asket, in zwanzig Zügen gezeichnet"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "pāpaśramaṇa"@en ;
    skos:definition "The portrait the seventeenth lecture draws of the man who has entered the order, learned the Law, received religious discipline and obtained the benefit of Bodhi, and afterwards begins to live as he likes. He sleeps, eats and drinks as much as he likes and lives comfortably; he despises the learning and discipline his teachers taught him; he hurts living beings, seeds and sprouts and does not control himself though he believes himself well-controlled; he uses bed, plank, chair, seat or duster without having wiped them; he walks in haste and without care; he inspects things carelessly; he is deceitful, talkative, arrogant and greedy; he perverts the truth and delights in quarrels; he sits down on a shaking seat wherever he lists; he sleeps with dusty feet; he eats milk, curds and what is made from milk and does not practise austerities; he eats after sunset and answers admonition with anger; he leaves his own teacher and follows heretical ones, changing his school continuously; he busies himself in another's house and lives by fortune-telling; he eats the food of his relations and reposes on the seat of the householder."@en ;
    skos:note "The nineteenth of the twenty marks is the one this holding should keep: living by fortune-telling. The transmission puts the practice of a divinatory art into its portrait of the failed ascetic, alongside sleeping too long and answering back, and the same practice appears in the prohibitions of the fifteenth lecture and in the catalogue of the Sūtrakṛtāṅga. Three different literary forms in three places, one rule."@en ;
    skos:note "Every one of the twenty marks is observable by somebody standing next to the man, which is exactly what the marks of the sinful ascetic at pmc:JainaLingamNaTrayate are not. The transmission therefore has a fully external portrait of the bad monk and a purely internal account of the monk whose practice fails, and it does not connect them."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVII.1-20 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstPapashramana a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Against becoming the bad ascetic"@en, "Gegen das Werden zum schlechten Asketen"@de ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaPapashramana ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:note "The lecture closes the warning with a judgement rather than a penalty: such a monk, who like the heretics does not protect himself from sins and who though having the appearance of a monk is the lowest among his worthy brethren, is despised in this world like poison, and he is nobody in this world and in that beyond."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XVII.20 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

#
# The arts, named at length in order to be forbidden
#

pmc:JainaNimittavidya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The divinatory arts the true monk does not live on"@en, "Die Wahrsagekünste, von denen der wahre Mönch nicht lebt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The list the fifteenth lecture gives, in a verse whose subject is what a monk must not profess or live on: divination from cuts and shreds, from sounds on the earth or in the air, from dreams, from diagrams, from sticks, from the properties of buildings, from changes in the body, and from the meaning of the cries of animals."@en ;
    skos:note "The translator remarks on what the list does not contain: there is no astrology in it, and he infers that Greek nativity had not yet risen to importance where and when the lecture was composed. The inference is his; the absence is the text's and is entered as such, because a catalogue of forbidden arts is also a catalogue of the arts that were there to forbid."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XV.7 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaCikitsavidya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The healing arts the true monk abstains from"@en, "Die Heilkünste, von denen sich der wahre Mönch enthält"@de ;
    skos:definition "The list that follows in the next verse of the same lecture: spells, roots, every kind of medical treatment, emetics, purgatives, fumigation, anointing of the eye, and bathing, together with the patient's lamentation and his consolation. He who abstains from all these things is a true monk."@en ;
    skos:note "The last two items are not treatments. Attending to the patient's lamentation and consoling him stand in the same prohibition as the emetic and the spell, which means the rule is against the whole office of the healer and not against the efficacy of any particular means."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XV.8 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaCautionAgainstNimittaAndCikitsa a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Against professing the arts and against healing"@en, "Gegen das Ausüben der Künste und gegen das Heilen"@de ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:JainaNimittavidya, pmc:JainaCikitsavidya ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:note "Repeated in a second work with a stated outcome rather than a definition: he who practises divination from bodily marks and dreams, who is well versed in augury and superstitious rites, who gains a sinful living by practising magic tricks, will have no refuge at the time of retribution."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XV.7-8 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)", "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XX.45 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaPapavidya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The catalogue of evil sciences"@en, "Der Katalog der bösen Wissenschaften"@de ;
    skos:definition "The longest list of operative arts in either volume, given in the second book of the Sūtrakṛtāṅga under the heading of men's success by occult sciences. Divination from terrestrial accidents, from strange phenomena, from dreams, from phenomena in the air, from changes in the body, from sounds, from mystical signs, from seeds; the interpretation of the marks of women, men, elephants, cows, partridges, cocks, ducks, quails, of wheels, parasols, shields, sticks, swords, precious stones and jewels; the art to make one happy or miserable, to make a woman pregnant, to deprive one of his wits; incantations; conjuring; oblations of substances; the martial arts; the courses of the moon, the sun, Venus and Jupiter; the falling of meteors; great conflagration; divination from wild animals, from the flight of crows, from showers of dust and from rain of blood; the Vaitālī and Ardhavaitālī arts; the art of casting people asleep and of opening doors; the arts of Caṇḍālas, Śabaras, Draviḍas, Kaliṅgas, Gauḍas and Gandhāras; and the spells for making somebody fall down, rise or yawn, for making him immovable or cling to something, for making him sick or sound, and for making him go forth, disappear or come."@en ;
    skos:note "Fifty-odd named capacities in three sentences, and the whole of it stands inside a prohibition. This is the shape in which this transmission carries the material the holding is looking for: not as a promise with a procedure but as an enumeration with a penalty. A later reader should not take the list for a Jain claim about what can be done; it is a Jain claim about what was being done."@en ;
    skos:note "Several of the arts named here are in the holding already from other transmissions, under their own names and with their own procedures. The overlap is at the level of the list only, and no convergence is asserted from it: the passage names the arts without describing any of them, so there is nothing here to match against a procedure."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra II.2.25-27 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmp:JainaPapavidyabhyasa a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Practising the arts for one's keep"@en, "Das Ausüben der Künste für den Unterhalt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The act the same passage condemns, and it condemns it under a stated condition: these and similar sciences are practised by some men for the sake of food, drink, clothes, a lodging, a bed, and various objects of pleasure."@en ;
    skos:note "The motive is in the sentence and it is what the sentence turns on. What is named is not the exercise of the art but living by it, and the verdict that follows — they practise a wrong science, the unworthy, the mistaken men — is passed on men so described."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra II.2.27 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaPapavidyaphala a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Born among the Asuras, and then deaf and dumb"@en, "Geburt unter den Asuras, danach taub und stumm"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the same passage states for the men who live by those sciences: after having died at their allotted time they will be born in some places inhabited by Asuras and evildoers, and when they are released therefrom they will again be born deaf and dumb, or night-blind."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated consequence in two stages, the second of them a named bodily condition. It is the clearest case in this file of a transmission naming what a practice produces, and what it names is a penalty. Held beside the holding's other yields for the sake of the comparison: the same arts that are promised elsewhere are here costed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra II.2.27 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaPapavidyaYieldsAsuratva a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Living by the arts produces the birth among Asuras"@en, "Das Leben von den Künsten bringt die Geburt unter den Asuras hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:JainaPapavidyabhyasa ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JainaPapavidyaphala ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    skos:note "Entered as a yielding claim although what is yielded is a penalty, because the form of the sentence is the form the class holds: this practice, carried out in this way, produces this. Nothing in the class requires the outcome to be desirable, and a holding that entered only the desirable ones would report the promises of the transmissions and not their claims."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra II.2.27 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaAbhiyogikabhavana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The disposition of the one who works spells for power"@en, "Der Zustand dessen, der Zauber um der Macht willen wirkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two of the dispositions the thirty-sixth lecture lists among those that fix a lower birth. Those who practise spells and besmear their body with ashes for the sake of pleasure, amusement, or power, realise the Abhiyogika disposition. And he who is continuously angry, and who puts his faith in prognostics, realises the Asuratva disposition."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition is again the motive and it is named three ways: pleasure, amusement, power. The second sentence puts faith in prognostics beside continual anger, which is a judgement on the client of the art and not only on its practitioner — the one place in what was read where consulting rather than performing is what is charged."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XXXVI.263 and XXXVI.265 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

#
# And the same transmission grants that the arts work
#

pmc:JainaNimittajnanaBhavishya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Those who have studied the arts know the future"@en, "Die die Künste studiert haben, wissen das Kommende"@de ;
    skos:definition "The premise the twelfth lecture of the first book uses against the philosophers who hold the world to be unreal: many men in this world who have studied astrology, the art of interpreting dreams, divination from diagrams, augury, divination from bodily marks and from portents, and the eight branches of divination from omens, know the future."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as a fact and not as a concession, and used as a premise: the edition read notes that this would be impossible if the whole world were unreal. The same transmission that forbids its monks to live by these arts and threatens the birth among Asuras to those who do, here grants that they yield knowledge of what is to come, and grants it in order to win a different argument."@en ;
    skos:note "That is not a contradiction and should not be entered as one. The prohibitions are about who may live by the arts and on what motive; this sentence is about whether the arts inform. A transmission can hold both, and this one does. What it costs the transmission is the easiest ground for the prohibition, which it therefore never takes."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.12.9 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaNimittaVivada a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Some forecasts are true and the prophecies of others prove wrong"@en, "Manche Vorhersagen treffen zu, die Prophezeiungen anderer erweisen sich als falsch"@de ;
    skos:definition "The opposing position the next verse reports and rejects: the opponents say that some forecasts are true and the prophecies of others prove wrong, and that therefore they do not study those sciences, but profess to know the world, fools though they be."@en ;
    skos:note "A discrimination argument reported inside a transmission and attributed to its opponents: the art is not to be studied because its results do not agree with one another. It is the only argument of that form anywhere in what was read, and this transmission is on the other side of it."@en ;
    skos:note "The reading is not secure and the insecurity is recorded rather than hidden. The edition notes a variant on which the line says instead that one must give up science altogether, and the translator marks the whole verse as the opponents' speech by a supplement of his own in brackets. What the node holds is what stands in the edition read, and a critical edition may move it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.12.10 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

#
# The argument against the austerities of others
#

pmc:JainaJalasiddhi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Perfection by salt, by water, by fire"@en, "Vollendung durch Salz, durch Wasser, durch Feuer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The three claims the seventh lecture of the first book reports of others before attacking them: some say that perfection is reached by abstaining from the seasoner of food, others by the use of cold water, that is by ablutions, and others again by tending a fire. The passage adds that those who hold the second touch water in the morning and evening, and that those who hold the third light a fire in the morning and evening."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as its opponent reports it, and the citation says so. No transmission of these practices has been run down in this holding; what stands is a Jain report of what others were doing, quoted closely enough that the argument against it can be checked against it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.7.12-13 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaJalasiddhiVivada a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether water or fire can perfect anyone"@en, "Ob Wasser oder Feuer irgendjemanden vollenden können"@de ;
    skos:definition "The refutation the same lecture mounts, and it is built by taking the claim at its word and reading a consequence off it. If perfection could be obtained by contact with water, many beings living in water must have reached perfection: fishes, tortoises, aquatic snakes, cormorants, otters, and the demons living in water. If water did wash off the impure Karman it must take off merit too. If water did wash off the sins of him who committed them, some would have obtained perfection who killed water-beings. And if perfection could be obtained through fire, mechanics also, who use fire, would be liberated. The lecture concludes that perfection cannot be established by such gratuitous assertions."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most transferable thing in either volume. The argument is not that the rival practice is impious; it is that the rival's own rule, applied without exception, entails an outcome in a class of beings who satisfy the rule by nature, and that the outcome is not there. This holding already carries the same instrument from the other end of Asia, in the first-century Chinese treatise that puts consequences of the day-choosing doctrine to its own practitioners. Two transmissions, no contact route between them at that date, one argument form."@en ;
    skos:note "It has the weakness of its strength, and the lecture does not notice it. The same reasoning would tell against the austerity this transmission itself prescribes, since beings who fast by nature are not reported as perfected either; the reply available to it is that the fast works by intention and not by contact, which is exactly the reply a defender of the ablutions would give. The argument therefore refutes a reading of the rival practice rather than the practice."@en ;
    skos:note "Both citations are the Jain text's, one for the claim and one for the refutation, so the dispute is reported by one side only and the graph holds no witness for the other. That is recorded rather than concealed, as at pmc:SuiyueSuoshiDispute, where the same situation arises for the same reason: the practice attacked has left no transmission this holding has opened."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:JainaJalasiddhi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.7.12-13 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)", "Sūtrakṛtāṅga Sūtra I.7.14-19 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

#
# Healing: a reported failure, and a rule of indifference to the outcome
#

pmc:JainaVaidyanamVifalata a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The physicians who knew spells and roots could not rid him of his pains"@en, "Die Ärzte, die Zauber und Wurzeln kannten, konnten ihn von seinen Schmerzen nicht befreien"@de ;
    skos:definition "The case the twentieth lecture reports in the first person. The best physicians came to his help, who cure by their medical art and by spells, who were versed in their science and well knew spells and roots; they tried to cure him according to the fourfold science which they had been taught, but they could not rid him of his pains. Nor could his father with all he possessed, nor his mother, brothers, sisters or wife. He then resolved that if he once got rid of these great pains he would become a houseless monk; while he thought so he fell asleep, and after that night his pains had vanished."@en ;
    skos:note "A named craft named as having failed, inside the tradition that reports the case, with the practitioners described as competent and the remedy attempted in full. That is a rarer thing in this holding than a claim of success, and it is rarer still that the recovery is then reported as following a resolution and a night's sleep rather than any treatment."@en ;
    skos:note "What the passage does not do is generalise. It is an argument for the helplessness of every protector, made in a lecture about the want of protection, and the physicians stand in it beside father, mother and wife. The failure of the craft is the premise and never the point, which is why it can be read as a report rather than as a polemic."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Uttarādhyayana Sūtra XX.21-33 (Jacobi, SBE 45, Oxford 1895; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 2010)" .

pmc:JainaVedanaPurvakarma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Neither pleased with the cure nor forbidding it"@en, "Weder erfreut über die Heilung noch sie verbietend"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rule the thirteenth lecture of the second book gives for the monk who is being treated: one should neither be pleased with nor prohibit it if another tries to cure him by pure charms, or by impure charms, or by digging up and cutting living bulbs, roots, rind or sprouts for the sake of a sick monk. The reason the lecture gives is that sensation is the result of former actions and that all sorts of living beings experience sensation."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule does not say that the charms fail; it says that the monk is to have no view about the outcome, because the sensation was determined before the treatment began. A doctrine of that shape removes every case from the reach of the question: a cure and a failure to cure are equally what the former actions required, and the transmission has said so in the sentence that gives the reason."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands beside the reported failure at pmc:JainaVaidyanamVifalata and the two do not sit easily together. There the craft is described as having been tried and having failed, which presupposes that an outcome was at stake; here no outcome is at stake at all. Both are entered because both are in the corpus, and the difference is in the literary form: a story admits a failure a doctrine cannot."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Jain ;
    dcterms:source "Ācārāṅga Sūtra II.13.22-23 (Jacobi, SBE 22, Oxford 1884; Motilal Banarsidass reprint, Delhi 1964)" .
